    "slug": "how-to-calculate-npl-ratio",
    "question": "How do I calculate the non-performing loans (NPL) ratio?",
    "cluster": "capital-and-risk",
    "clusterName": "Capital Strength and Asset Quality",
    "intentType": "procedural",
    "shortAnswer": "The NPL ratio is calculated by dividing non-performing loans (loans 90+ days past due or on non-accrual) by total loans, expressing the percentage of the loan portfolio that is no longer performing as expected",
    "fullAnswer": "The formula for the NPL ratio is:\n\nNPL Ratio = Non-Performing Loans / Total Loans\n\nThe result is expressed as a percentage.\n\nStep-by-step calculation:\n\n1. Determine non-performing loans. Non-performing loans consist of two categories: loans that are 90 or more days past due and still accruing interest, and loans placed on non-accrual status. Non-accrual loans are those where the bank has stopped recognizing interest income because full collection of principal and interest is no longer expected. These two categories are typically disclosed separately in the bank's financial statements.\n2. Find total loans on the balance sheet. Use total loans before the deduction for the allowance for credit losses (gross loans) for consistency, though some analysts use net loans (gross loans minus the allowance). The key is to be consistent across banks and time periods.\n3. Divide non-performing loans by total loans.\n\nWorked example: A bank discloses $45 million in non-accrual loans and $8 million in loans 90+ days past due and still accruing. Total loans are $5.2 billion. Non-performing loans = $45M + $8M = $53 million. NPL ratio = $53M / $5.2B = 1.02%.\n\nWhat qualifies as non-performing: The standard definition includes loans past due 90 days or more (regardless of accrual status) and loans on non-accrual. However, some banks and analysts use a stricter definition that includes only non-accrual loans, excluding the 90+ days past due and still accruing category. When comparing NPL ratios across banks, verify which definition each bank uses in its disclosures.\n\nLoans past due 30 to 89 days are not included in the non-performing category, but they are an important early warning indicator. Banks disclose these \"early-stage delinquencies\" in their aging schedules, and a rising trend in 30-to-89-day delinquencies often precedes increases in the NPL ratio.\n\nTroubled debt restructurings (TDRs): Prior to the adoption of ASU 2022-02 (effective for most banks in 2023), loans that were restructured as TDRs were often classified as non-performing even if the borrower was current under the modified terms. Under the updated standard, the TDR designation was eliminated, and these loans are evaluated under the general credit loss framework. This change may have modestly reduced reported NPL levels at some banks.\n\nWhere to find the inputs: Non-performing loan data is disclosed in the bank's 10-K and 10-Q in the credit quality section, typically in tables showing loans by delinquency status and accrual status. Many banks also disclose non-performing loan totals in their quarterly earnings releases and investor presentations. Call report data (available through the FFIEC) provides standardized non-performing loan figures for all FDIC-insured institutions.\n\nCommon calculation mistakes: Including OREO or other foreclosed assets in the numerator (that would be the NPA ratio, not the NPL ratio). Using net loans (after the allowance deduction) inconsistently across banks. Comparing NPL ratios across banks that define non-performing loans differently without adjusting.",
