    "slug": "how-to-calculate-loan-loss-reserve-ratio",
    "question": "How do I calculate the loan loss reserve ratio?",
    "cluster": "capital-and-risk",
    "clusterName": "Capital Strength and Asset Quality",
    "intentType": "procedural",
    "shortAnswer": "The loan loss reserve ratio is calculated by dividing the allowance for credit losses by total loans, measuring what percentage of the loan portfolio the bank has reserved against for expected future losses",
    "fullAnswer": "The formula for the loan loss reserve ratio is:\n\nLoan Loss Reserve Ratio = Allowance for Credit Losses / Total Loans\n\nThe result is expressed as a percentage.\n\nStep-by-step calculation:\n\n1. Find the allowance for credit losses (ACL) on the balance sheet. This appears as a contra-asset that reduces the gross loan figure to arrive at net loans. It may be labeled \"allowance for loan and lease losses\" (ALLL) in older filings, though the terminology shifted to ACL with the adoption of CECL.\n2. Find total loans on the balance sheet. Use gross loans (before the allowance deduction) in the denominator. Total loans may be labeled \"loans and leases, net of unearned income\" or similar.\n3. Divide the allowance by total loans.\n\nWorked example: A bank has an allowance for credit losses of $72 million and total gross loans of $5.2 billion. Loan loss reserve ratio = $72M / $5.2B = 1.38%.\n\nHow the allowance is built and used: The allowance is increased through provision expense (a charge on the income statement) and decreased when loans are charged off. Recoveries on previously charged-off loans add back to the allowance. The allowance rollforward in the financial statements shows each of these components: Beginning allowance + Provision - Charge-offs + Recoveries = Ending allowance.\n\nCECL impact on reserve levels: Under the CECL standard (ASC 326), banks estimate lifetime expected credit losses at origination, which generally produces higher reserve levels than the prior incurred-loss model, particularly for longer-duration loan portfolios. When comparing reserve ratios across time periods, the shift to CECL (effective 2020 for large banks, 2023 for smaller banks) represents a structural increase that does not necessarily indicate worsening credit quality.\n\nReserve ratio by loan category: Banks disclose the allowance allocation by loan type (commercial real estate, C&I, residential mortgage, consumer, etc.) in the notes to financial statements. This breakdown reveals which loan categories management views as carrying the most risk. For example, a bank might reserve 0.50% against its residential mortgage portfolio but 2.50% against its construction loan portfolio, reflecting the different risk profiles.\n\nRelationship to reserve coverage: The loan loss reserve ratio measures reserves relative to the total loan portfolio. The reserve coverage ratio measures reserves relative to non-performing loans specifically. A bank can have a moderate reserve ratio (1.3%) but strong reserve coverage (150%) if its NPLs are low relative to its total portfolio. Both perspectives are useful for different aspects of credit analysis.\n\nRelationship to provision expense: If the reserve ratio is stable over time while the loan portfolio is growing, provision expense must be keeping pace to maintain the ratio. A declining reserve ratio during a period of loan growth may indicate the bank is not provisioning adequately, or it may reflect improving credit quality that justifies lower reserves.\n\nWhere to find the inputs: The allowance for credit losses is on the balance sheet and in the notes to financial statements. The allowance rollforward table in the 10-K and 10-Q provides the full detail of provisions, charge-offs, and recoveries. Total gross loans are on the balance sheet.",
