    "slug": "how-to-calculate-reserve-coverage-ratio",
    "question": "How do I calculate the reserve coverage ratio?",
    "cluster": "capital-and-risk",
    "clusterName": "Capital Strength and Asset Quality",
    "intentType": "procedural",
    "shortAnswer": "The reserve coverage ratio is calculated by dividing the allowance for credit losses by non-performing loans, measuring how many dollars of reserves the bank holds for each dollar of known problem loans",
    "fullAnswer": "The formula for the reserve coverage ratio is:\n\nReserve Coverage Ratio = Allowance for Credit Losses / Non-Performing Loans\n\nThe result is expressed as a percentage. A ratio of 150% means the bank has $1.50 in reserves for every $1.00 of non-performing loans.\n\nStep-by-step calculation:\n\n1. Find the allowance for credit losses (ACL) on the balance sheet.\n2. Calculate non-performing loans: non-accrual loans plus loans 90+ days past due and still accruing. These figures are in the credit quality disclosures in 10-K and 10-Q filings.\n3. Divide the allowance by non-performing loans.\n\nWorked example: A bank has an allowance for credit losses of $72 million and non-performing loans of $53 million. Reserve coverage ratio = $72M / $53M = 135.8%. The bank has reserved $1.36 for each dollar of non-performing loans.\n\nWhy 100% coverage matters: If all non-performing loans resulted in total loss (zero recovery), reserve coverage of 100% means the bank could absorb those losses entirely from its existing allowance without charging earnings or equity. In practice, most non-performing loans recover some value through collateral liquidation, restructuring, or eventual repayment, so coverage below 100% does not automatically mean the bank is under-provisioned. However, coverage significantly below 100% should prompt investigation into the collateral backing the non-performing loans and the bank's loss severity assumptions.\n\nTypical ranges: For U.S. banks during normal economic conditions, reserve coverage ratios typically range from 80% to 200%. Banks with well-collateralized non-performing loans (such as commercial real estate with strong collateral values) may appropriately maintain coverage around 80% to 100%. Banks with significant unsecured non-performing exposures (credit cards, personal loans) should generally maintain higher coverage because recovery rates are lower. Coverage above 200% may indicate either very conservative provisioning or a very small NPL base.\n\nCECL and reserve coverage: Under CECL, the allowance covers estimated lifetime losses on the entire loan portfolio, not just non-performing loans. This means the allowance includes reserves for loans that are currently performing but are expected to experience some level of loss over their remaining life. As a result, the reserve coverage ratio under CECL tends to be higher than it was under the prior incurred-loss model, because the allowance now covers a broader scope of expected losses.\n\nEdge case when NPLs are very low: When non-performing loans approach zero, the reserve coverage ratio can become very large (500%, 1,000%, or higher) and loses practical meaning. In these situations, the loan loss reserve ratio (allowance divided by total loans) is a more informative measure of provisioning adequacy.\n\nWhere to find the inputs: The allowance for credit losses is on the balance sheet. Non-performing loan figures are in the credit quality disclosures. Many banks report the reserve coverage ratio directly in their earnings releases and investor presentations.",
