    "slug": "what-are-money-center-banks",
    "question": "What are money center banks?",
    "cluster": "bank-types",
    "clusterName": "Understanding Bank Types",
    "intentType": "definitional",
    "shortAnswer": "Money center banks are the largest banking institutions, typically with hundreds of billions to trillions of dollars in assets, operating nationally and globally with diversified business lines that extend well beyond traditional deposit-taking and lending",
    "fullAnswer": "Money center banks are the largest and most complex financial institutions in the banking system. While there is no single formal definition, the term generally refers to banks with total assets exceeding $250 billion that operate across national and international markets, maintain significant capital markets and trading operations, and serve as core participants in the financial system's payment and settlement infrastructure.\n\nThe business model of a money center bank is fundamentally different from that of a community or regional bank. Revenue comes from a broad mix of sources: net interest income from a massive loan and securities portfolio, investment banking and advisory fees, trading revenue from fixed income, equities, and derivatives, transaction processing and payment services, wealth and asset management fees, credit card operations, and custody and clearing services. This diversification means that non-interest income often represents 40% to 60% or more of total revenue, compared to 10% to 25% for a typical community bank.\n\nMoney center banks operate under a more intensive regulatory framework than smaller institutions. Those designated as Global Systemically Important Banks (GSIBs) by the Financial Stability Board face additional capital surcharges, enhanced liquidity requirements, resolution planning mandates (commonly called \"living wills\"), and regular stress testing through the Federal Reserve's Comprehensive Capital Analysis and Review (CCAR) process. These requirements add compliance costs but also provide a degree of implicit stability assurance that smaller banks do not receive.\n\nFrom an analytical perspective, evaluating money center banks requires a different toolkit than evaluating community or regional banks. Standard metrics like net interest margin and efficiency ratio still apply but must be interpreted differently. Money center banks typically report lower net interest margins (often 1.50% to 2.50%) because their asset base includes lower-yielding trading assets and securities alongside loans. Their efficiency ratios may appear elevated because capital markets and wealth management businesses are more compensation-intensive than traditional banking. Segment-level analysis, which examines each business line separately, is often necessary to understand where value is being created.\n\nValuation of money center banks also differs. Price-to-tangible-book-value ratios for these institutions reflect not just the banking franchise but also the value of the capital markets platform, the global payments network, and the asset management business. These non-lending businesses may justify premiums to tangible book value that would be difficult to support for a bank dependent solely on spread income.",
