    "slug": "what-are-super-regional-banks",
    "question": "What are super-regional banks?",
    "cluster": "bank-types",
    "clusterName": "Understanding Bank Types",
    "intentType": "definitional",
    "shortAnswer": "Super-regional banks are large multi-state institutions, typically with $50 billion to $500 billion or more in assets, that operate across broad geographic regions with a business model more complex than traditional community banking but less diversified than money center banks",
    "fullAnswer": "Super-regional banks occupy the space between traditional regional banks and the largest money center institutions. The term describes banks that have grown beyond a single state or local region, usually through a combination of organic growth and acquisitions, to serve customers across a broad multi-state footprint. While there is no precise asset threshold, super-regionals generally have between $50 billion and $500 billion or more in total assets.\n\nThe business model of a super-regional bank blends traditional commercial banking with a growing set of specialized services. Commercial and industrial lending, commercial real estate, retail banking, and mortgage lending form the core. Many super-regionals have built significant wealth management and trust operations, treasury management platforms, and in some cases capital markets capabilities such as fixed income sales and trading or loan syndication. This broader product set differentiates them from pure community lenders while remaining more focused on banking fundamentals than money center institutions with global investment banking operations.\n\nSuper-regional banks occupy a distinct regulatory position. Banks with assets above $100 billion are subject to the Federal Reserve's enhanced prudential standards, which include stress testing requirements, more stringent capital and liquidity rules, and resolution planning. These requirements impose meaningful compliance costs but also discipline risk management in ways that can benefit long-term shareholders. Banks between $50 billion and $100 billion face a somewhat lighter version of these requirements, though the specific thresholds and standards have been adjusted by legislation and rulemaking over time.\n\nFor investors, super-regional banks offer a middle-ground investment profile. They provide more diversification and scale than community banks, with access to deeper management talent, broader product capabilities, and the operational leverage that comes with a larger asset base. At the same time, they remain more transparent and easier to analyze than money center banks, whose global operations, trading books, and complex derivative portfolios can be difficult to evaluate from public filings alone.\n\nPerformance metrics for super-regionals typically fall between community bank and money center bank ranges. Net interest margins are generally narrower than community banks (often 2.50% to 3.50%) because of a larger securities portfolio and more wholesale funding. Efficiency ratios tend to be more favorable (50% to 60%) due to scale advantages. Non-interest income usually represents 25% to 40% of revenue, reflecting the more diversified product mix.\n\nSuper-regional banks have historically been active acquirers, using mergers to expand into new markets and achieve the scale needed to compete with larger institutions. This acquisition activity means many super-regionals carry meaningful amounts of goodwill on their balance sheets, making the distinction between book value and tangible book value particularly important for valuation.",
