    "slug": "mutual-vs-stock-bank",
    "question": "What is the difference between a mutual bank and a stock bank?",
    "cluster": "bank-types",
    "clusterName": "Understanding Bank Types",
    "intentType": "comparative",
    "shortAnswer": "A mutual bank is owned by its depositors with no publicly traded shares, while a stock bank is owned by shareholders who hold equity in the institution, and this ownership structure affects governance, capital raising, and investment opportunities",
    "fullAnswer": "The distinction between mutual and stock ownership is one of the most fundamental structural differences in banking. It determines who owns the institution, how it is governed, and whether investors can participate as shareholders.\n\nA mutual bank (also called a mutual savings bank or mutual savings institution) is owned by its depositors. There are no shares of stock. Each depositor has a proportional ownership interest in the institution, though this interest is not represented by a tradeable security and has no market value independent of the deposit account. The board of directors is elected by depositors, and any profits are either retained to build capital or returned to depositors through higher interest rates or lower fees. Mutual banks cannot raise equity capital by issuing stock, which limits their options for funding growth or recovering from losses. Their primary source of new capital is retained earnings.\n\nA stock bank is organized as a corporation with shares of stock that represent ownership. These shares may be publicly traded on a stock exchange, traded over the counter, or closely held by a small group of private investors. The board of directors is elected by shareholders, who benefit from dividends and share price appreciation. Stock banks can raise capital by issuing new shares, giving them greater financial flexibility than mutual institutions.\n\nFor investors, only stock banks offer direct investment opportunities. Mutual banks do not have publicly traded shares and cannot be purchased through a brokerage account. However, mutual banks become investable when they convert to stock form, a process known as a mutual-to-stock conversion or, when done through a mutual holding company structure, an MHC conversion. These conversions have historically attracted significant investor interest because the IPO shares are often priced at a discount to the bank's pro forma tangible book value, creating a built-in margin of safety for initial investors.\n\nFrom an analytical standpoint, mutual and stock banks face the same fundamental banking economics. They make loans, gather deposits, and manage credit risk using the same tools. The primary differences are in capital planning (mutual banks cannot issue equity), corporate governance (depositor control versus shareholder control), and strategic flexibility (stock banks can more easily pursue acquisitions using stock as currency).\n\nThe number of mutual banks in the United States has declined steadily over several decades as institutions have converted to stock form or merged with stock banks. As of recent years, several hundred mutual institutions remain, primarily community-focused savings banks and savings institutions concentrated in the Northeast and Midwest.",
