    "slug": "what-is-concentration-risk",
    "question": "What is concentration risk in banking?",
    "cluster": "advanced",
    "clusterName": "Advanced Bank Analysis",
    "intentType": "definitional",
    "shortAnswer": "Concentration risk is the potential for outsized losses when a bank's lending, funding, or revenue is heavily dependent on a single borrower, industry, geography, or product type, and it is one of the most common risk factors in community bank failures",
    "fullAnswer": "Concentration risk arises when a bank's exposure is not sufficiently diversified across borrowers, industries, geographies, or product types. A concentrated portfolio performs well when the concentrated segment performs well, but it can generate severe losses when conditions in that segment deteriorate. Historically, concentration risk has been a leading contributor to bank failures and material credit losses.\n\nLoan concentration is the most commonly discussed form. The major categories include commercial real estate (CRE) concentration, which regulators monitor against specific capital-based thresholds; industry concentration, where a significant share of loans is extended to a single economic sector such as agriculture, energy, or healthcare; borrower concentration, where a small number of large credit relationships represent a disproportionate share of the loan portfolio; and geographic concentration, where the bank's lending is confined to a single community or metropolitan area whose economic fortunes drive portfolio performance.\n\nFunding concentration is a less visible but equally important risk. A bank that relies on a small number of large depositors, brokered deposits, or wholesale funding sources faces liquidity risk if those funding sources withdraw simultaneously. The events at several banks in recent years illustrated how concentrated and potentially volatile deposit bases can create rapid liquidity stress, even at institutions with sound asset quality.\n\nRevenue concentration can also create vulnerability. A bank that derives a large share of its fee income from a single business line, such as mortgage banking, faces revenue volatility when conditions in that business change. A bank dependent on a single market for its lending and deposit gathering is effectively a leveraged bet on that market's economic health.\n\nRegulators evaluate concentration risk as part of the examination process and expect banks with significant concentrations to have formal policies, board-approved limits, and stress testing programs that assess the potential impact of adverse scenarios on the concentrated exposure. Banks that manage concentration risk well can earn attractive returns from specialized expertise in a particular lending category, while banks that accumulate concentration without commensurate risk management may face regulatory criticism and elevated loss risk.\n\nInvestors can assess concentration risk through the loan composition disclosures in the 10-K and Call Report, the geographic distribution of lending, the deposit concentration data available in Call Report schedules, and management's discussion of concentration management in the annual report.",
