    "slug": "evaluating-deposit-franchise",
    "question": "How do I evaluate a bank's deposit franchise?",
    "cluster": "advanced",
    "clusterName": "Advanced Bank Analysis",
    "intentType": "strategic",
    "shortAnswer": "Evaluating a deposit franchise involves analyzing the mix of deposit types, the cost of deposits relative to peers, the stability and granularity of the deposit base, and the percentage of non-interest-bearing deposits, which collectively determine the bank's funding advantage",
    "fullAnswer": "The deposit franchise is often described as the most valuable asset a bank owns, even though it does not appear as an asset on the balance sheet. A strong deposit franchise provides low-cost, stable funding that enables the bank to earn wider net interest margins and sustain profitability through different interest rate environments.\n\nDeposit mix is the starting point for evaluation. Deposits are categorized as non-interest-bearing demand deposits (checking accounts that pay no interest), interest-bearing demand and savings deposits (including money market accounts), and time deposits (certificates of deposit with fixed terms and rates). Non-interest-bearing deposits are the most valuable because they have zero explicit cost. A bank where 25% to 35% or more of total deposits are non-interest-bearing has a meaningful funding cost advantage over a bank where non-interest-bearing deposits represent 10% to 15% of the total.\n\nThe cost of deposits, calculated as total interest expense on deposits divided by average total deposits, is the most direct measure of funding cost. Banks with strong deposit franchises typically report deposit costs well below the federal funds rate and below peers of similar size. The deposit beta, which measures how much the bank's deposit costs increase for each unit increase in market interest rates, reveals the franchise's rate sensitivity. A low deposit beta indicates pricing power: the bank can hold deposit rates relatively steady even as market rates rise.\n\nDeposit stability and granularity matter for liquidity risk. A deposit base composed of millions of small retail accounts is inherently more stable than one dependent on a smaller number of large commercial or institutional accounts. Regulators and analysts distinguish between \"core deposits\" (typically retail and small business accounts that are stable and rate-insensitive) and \"non-core deposits\" (including brokered deposits, large time deposits, and listing service deposits that are more rate-sensitive and mobile). A higher ratio of core deposits to total deposits indicates greater funding stability.\n\nDeposit growth trends reveal whether the franchise is strengthening or eroding. Organic deposit growth that keeps pace with or exceeds loan growth indicates a self-funding bank that does not need to rely on wholesale borrowings or aggressive rate competition. Declining deposit balances or a shift from non-interest-bearing to interest-bearing accounts may signal competitive pressure or customer attrition.\n\nThe deposits-to-assets ratio measures the extent to which deposits fund the bank's balance sheet. Most well-funded community and regional banks maintain deposits-to-assets ratios of 75% to 85%. Ratios below this range may indicate reliance on Federal Home Loan Bank advances or other wholesale funding, which is typically more expensive and less stable than core deposits.\n\nIn acquisition valuation, the deposit franchise is often valued through the core deposit premium, expressed as a percentage of core deposits. Acquirers willingly pay 5% to 15% or more of core deposits as a premium in bank acquisitions, reflecting the long-term funding value that a strong deposit base provides.",
